  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1159 PM CDT FRI OCT 16 2026\n\nThis evening the cold front has moved southeast of Central\nAlabama and extends along the Eastern Seaboard to the Georgia\nCoast, then southwest across the Gulf. A large ridge stretches\nacross much of Conus with a 1036-37mb center stretched across\nportions of New Mexico, the Texas Panhandle, and northeast toward\nEastern Nebraska. Breezy and gusty at times NRLY winds will\ncontinue for the remainder of tonight and into the daytime on\nSaturday with tight surface pressure gradients behind the front.\nWinds should begin to taper down on Saturday night as the ridge\ncenter shifts to over Indiana/Ohio and gradients relax across the\nDeep South. Cool fall readings are on tap with lows in the 40s\nfor most this week. We could even see a few upper 30s in a few of\nthe colder northern locales and valleys by Sunday night. Highs\nwill be generally in the 60s over the weekend. While overall\nEastern Conus ridging continues this week, it does weaken a\nlittle. As such, our airmass should begin to moderate as we head\nthrough the upcoming week as our flow becomes more northeast to\neasterly. Look for highs by Monday to be seasonal in the 70s,\nwith 80s creeping back for some by Wednesday. With that said, we\nwill remain rain free with very little moisture available for\nmuch cloud cover much less rain.\n\n08",
